{"answer":"No. The conclusion that √2 is rational is false. The parity argument is a contradiction proof: if √2 were rational, choose a representation with least positive denominator; then a²=2b² forces both a and b even, producing a representation with a smaller positive denominator. Thus √2 is irrational. This revised draft supplies the previously missing reduction-to-lowest-terms argument via the well-ordering principle.","approaches":[{"description":"Assume √2 is rational, choose an integer representation with least positive denominator, and use parity to contradict that minimality.","id":"minimal-denominator","outcome":"candidate","reason":"It derives the required lowest-terms condition rather than assuming an unproved reduction principle."}],"change_log":["Replaced the unsupported inference from ‘no lowest-terms representation’ to irrationality with a minimal-positive-denominator construction.","Removed the unnecessary gcd condition: divisibility of both numerator and denominator by 2 directly contradicts denominator minimality.","Retained and made explicit the parity calculations establishing that an integer with even square is even.","The audit objection concerning reduction to lowest terms is addressed; no unresolved mathematical objection remains."],"claims":[{"basis":"local_assumption","basis_reference":"Temporary contradiction assumption.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":["step-contradiction"],"id":"claim-rational-assumption","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":["step-positive-denominator","step-minimal-denominator","step-square-equation","step-a-even","step-b-square-even","step-b-even","step-halved-representation","step-contradiction"],"statement":"Assume, for contradiction, that √2 is rational.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived from the definition of a rational number under claim-rational-assumption.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-rational-assumption"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-integer-representation","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, √2=a/b for some integers a,b with b>0.","step_ids":["step-positive-denominator"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"standard_result","basis_reference":"Well-ordering principle for positive integers, applied in step-minimal-denominator to the nonempty set of positive denominators from claim-integer-representation.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-integer-representation"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-minimal-denominator","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, choose integers a,b with b>0 and √2=a/b such that b is least among all positive integer denominators of representations of √2.","step_ids":["step-minimal-denominator"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by squaring the selected equality and multiplying by b², with b>0.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-minimal-denominator"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-square-equation","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For the selected integers, a²=2b².","step_ids":["step-square-equation"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by contraposition using the explicit odd-square calculation in step-a-even.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-square-equation"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-a-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For the selected integers, a is even.","step_ids":["step-a-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by substituting a=2k into a²=2b².","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-a-even","claim-square-equation"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-b-square-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For the selected integers, b² is even.","step_ids":["step-b-square-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by contraposition using the explicit odd-square calculation in step-b-even.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-b-square-even"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-b-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For the selected integers, b is even.","step_ids":["step-b-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The contradiction in step-contradiction discharges claim-rational-assumption.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-rational-assumption","claim-minimal-denominator","claim-a-even","claim-b-even"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-irrational","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"√2 is irrational.","step_ids":["step-contradiction"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-positive-denominator","justification":"By the definition of rational, √2=p/q for integers p,q with q≠0. If q<0, replace p,q by -p,-q; the denominator is then positive.","statement":"There is at least one representation √2=a/b with integers a,b and b>0."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-positive-denominator"],"id":"step-minimal-denominator","justification":"Apply the well-ordering principle to the nonempty set of positive integer denominators occurring in representations of √2.","statement":"Choose a representation √2=a/b whose positive denominator b is least."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-minimal-denominator"],"id":"step-square-equation","justification":"Square √2=a/b to obtain 2=a²/b². Since b>0, multiplying by b² gives a²=2b².","statement":"a²=2b²."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-square-equation"],"id":"step-a-even","justification":"The equation makes a² even. If a were odd, a=2k+1 for an integer k, and a²=4k²+4k+1=2(2k²+2k)+1, which is odd. Hence a is even.","statement":"a=2k for some integer k."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-square-equation","step-a-even"],"id":"step-b-square-even","justification":"Substitute a=2k into a²=2b²: 4k²=2b², hence b²=2k².","statement":"b² is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-b-square-even"],"id":"step-b-even","justification":"If b were odd, writing b=2l+1 would give b²=2(2l²+2l)+1, which is odd, contrary to step-b-square-even.","statement":"b=2l for some integer l."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-minimal-denominator","step-a-even","step-b-even"],"id":"step-halved-representation","justification":"With a=2k and b=2l, √2=a/b=(2k)/(2l)=k/l. Since b=2l>0, l>0, and l=b/2<b.","statement":"√2 has a positive-denominator representation with denominator smaller than b."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-minimal-denominator","step-halved-representation"],"id":"step-contradiction","justification":"Step-halved-representation contradicts the minimality of b selected in step-minimal-denominator. Therefore the temporary rationality assumption is false.","statement":"√2 is irrational."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
